\newlabel{sec:primitive}{{3.3}{20}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The object-relative push primitive, drawn to scale over a top-down T-block. Panel (a) shows the approach placement in the object frame: the contact point is set by a radial offset $r$, sampled from the band between $0.04\,\text  {m}$ and $0.12\,\text  {m}$ from the object centre (dashed circles), and an approach angle $\phi $, one of 21 directions (grey spokes); the green points are the resulting grid of candidate contact points. The band is sized so that it encloses the object, whose outline reaches about $0.10\,\text  {m}$ from the tracked centre. Panel (b) shows the push itself in the world frame, from a representative contact point: the push direction $\theta $ is one of 21 headings (blue arrows), and the push length is one of 21 values from 0 to $0.20\,\text  {m}$ (marked along one heading). Each of the four parameters is discretised into 21 bins, so the action is a four-dimensional choice over a fixed grid rather than a continuous command.}}{22}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:primitive_bins}{{2}{22}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{loa}{\contentsline {algorithm}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Decode and execute one object-relative push primitive.}}{22}{}\protected@file@percent }
